# Formatting only, and only on what is staged — plus one generated file.
#
# `format-check` is the single most frequent CI failure in this repository —
# more than the next three causes combined — and every one of them is the same
# story: a file that Prettier would have fixed in milliseconds instead costs a
# push, a runner, and several minutes before it says so.
#
# Deliberately narrow. Linting and typechecking are not here: they are slower,
# they have not been failing, and a pre-commit hook that takes long enough to
# be annoying is a pre-commit hook people run with --no-verify.
#
# Fixtures are safe: Prettier honours .prettierignore even for the explicit
# paths lint-staged hands it, and `--ignore-unknown` skips file types it has
# no parser for (images, .ico, .nvmrc) rather than failing the commit.
pnpm lint-staged

# `packages/cli/rules.json` is generated from the rule registry and read by the
# documentation site, which cannot import the package (invariant I3). Regenerate
# it here so a rule change and its published description land in the same
# commit, rather than in a CI failure minutes later or — as happened before this
# existed — in nothing at all, leaving the site describing a rule the engine
# stopped having.
#
# Conditional and quiet: it runs only when a rule file is actually staged, which
# is a handful of commits a month, and costs about a second when it does. The
# same narrowness the rest of this hook is built on.
#
# Not the guarantee. `--no-verify` skips all of this, so `catalog.test.ts`
# compares the committed file against the registry in the suite CI already runs.
# The hook is the fast path; the test is the one that cannot be skipped.
if git diff --cached --name-only | grep -q '^packages/cli/src/lib/rules/'; then
  pnpm --filter @goflag/cli generate:catalog >/dev/null
  git add packages/cli/rules.json
fi

# `packages/cli/flags.json` is the same arrangement for the CLI's flag table,
# and it exists for the same reason: the reference page on the site cannot
# import this package either, so it kept thirty flags by hand — and a
# documentation audit found four drifts in that copy, including a flag it did
# not mention at all.
#
# `flags.json` is not the guarantee any more than `rules.json` is:
# `src/lib/flags/catalog.test.ts` compares the committed file against the table,
# in the suite CI runs, which `--no-verify` cannot skip.
if git diff --cached --name-only | grep -q '^packages/cli/src/lib/flags/'; then
  pnpm --filter @goflag/cli generate:flags >/dev/null
  git add packages/cli/flags.json
fi
